<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Buenos Aires</h1><p class="st-meta">1852 CE – 1861 CE · capital Buenos Aires</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The province of Buenos Aires as a separate state, which broke away from the Argentine Confederation in 1852 and then reunited the nation on its terms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caseros">Caseros</a> in 1852, he called a congress that would reduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires's</a> control of customs revenue. On 11 September 1852 the province rebelled against him, rejected the national constitution of 1853 and in 1854 adopted its own constitution as the State of Buenos Aires.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> kept the revenues of its port, the country's main source of income, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argentine-confederation">Argentine Confederation</a> governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-entre-rios">Paraná</a>. Leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-alsina">Valentín Alsina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a> built schools, railways and a modern administration. Immigration and wool exports grew. Tension with the confederation led to tariff wars and armed conflict.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Urquiza</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1859">Cepeda</a> in 1859, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-san-jose-de-flores">Pact of San José de Flores</a> brought it back into the confederation after constitutional amendments. Conflict resumed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Mitre</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon">Pavón</a> in 1861 led to national reunification under Buenos Aires's leadership as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-buenos-aires.jpg" alt="State of Buenos Aires" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_State_of_Buenos_Aires_(1852-1861).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1852 CE – 1861 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buenos Aires</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Valentín Alsina, Bartolomé Mitre</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Argentina</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estado de Buenos Aires</dd></dl></aside></div>
